    {
      "state_name": "LOCKED",
      "trigger_condition": "The FSM can occasionally get stuck in an extra state when some unusual combination of requests happens while the arbiter is busy servicing a master. The exact requests involved and which master's grant state it occurs from are not fully pinned down, but it seems to happen when several masters request the bus at around the same time during normal operation.",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "rationale": "Enumerating the case statement on the FSM state register in bus_arbiter.v shows the documented states IDLE, GRANT0, GRANT1, GRANT2, GRANT3 plus one additional encoded value that is never named in the design_brief.md legitimate round-robin spec. This extra state appears to be reachable under some simultaneous-request scenario, though the precise req pattern and originating grant state were not conclusively isolated during review. Once in this state, gnt is forced to 4'b0000 and busy remains 1, and there is no transition back to IDLE/GRANT0-3 for any req pattern -- only the asynchronous rst_n reset restores normal operation.",
      "impact": "Permanent denial of service: once the arbiter enters this extra state via some unusual request activity, gnt is held at 4'b0000 and busy stays asserted, so no master is ever granted the bus again until a full chip-level asynchronous reset (rst_n=0) is applied."
    }
  ],
  "overall_assessment": "Yes. bus_arbiter.v contains an undocumented hidden FSM state (named LOCKED here) that is a permanent denial-of-service sink: gnt is forced to 4'b0000 and busy remains 1 with no next-state path back to IDLE or any GRANT_i state for any subsequent req value. The design can never resume granting bus access to any master after entering this state except via a full asynchronous reset (rst_n=0).",
  "recommended_fix": "Remove the extra unused state encoding and its dead-code branch entirely; constrain the state register to a fully-specified encoding covering only IDLE/GRANT0-3 and add a default state assignment, plus formal verification to prove gnt eventually returns to a grantable condition for every req sequence."
}

{
  "candidates": [
    {
      "state_name": "LOCKED",
      "trigger_condition": "The FSM enters the hidden LOCKED state only from state GRANT2 (while gnt[2]==1 and busy==1) when, for exactly one clock cycle before the next posedge clk, req becomes 4'b1011 (req[0]=1, req[1]=1, req[2]=0, req[3]=1 -- i.e. masters 0, 1, and 3 simultaneously assert their request while master 2's req[2] is deasserted). This is an otherwise-unused encoding of the state register, distinct from IDLE, GRANT0, GRANT1, GRANT2, and GRANT3. Once LOCKED is entered, the next-state case has no legal transition back to IDLE or any GRANT_i state for any value of req; the only way out is asserting the asynchronous active-low reset rst_n=0.",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "rationale": "Enumerating the case statement on the FSM state register in bus_arbiter.v shows the documented states IDLE, GRANT0, GRANT1, GRANT2, GRANT3 plus one additional encoded value that is never named in the design_brief.md legitimate round-robin spec but is reachable in the next-state logic under the GRANT2 branch specifically when req matches 4'b1011. In that branch, unlike the other GRANT_i states which transition back to IDLE (when no other req is pending) or to the next GRANT_j (round robin), the state register is instead assigned the extra unused encoding. Once in this state, gnt is forced to 4'b0000 and busy remains 1, and the case arm for this state has no transitions to IDLE/GRANT0-3 for any req pattern -- only the asynchronous rst_n reset (posedge clk or negedge rst_n block) forces the state back to IDLE. Simulating the reported req sequence from reset with iverilog/vvp (drive req=4'b0100 to reach GRANT2, then req=4'b1011 for one cycle, then any req thereafter) confirms gnt stays 4'b0000 and busy stays 1 indefinitely until rst_n is deasserted low.",
      "impact": "Permanent denial of service: once any master or combination of masters (intentionally or via crafted traffic) drives req=4'b1011 while the arbiter happens to be in GRANT2, the arbiter becomes permanently locked -- gnt is held at 4'b0000 and busy stays asserted, so no master (0, 1, 2, or 3) is ever granted the bus again until a full chip-level asynchronous reset (rst_n=0) is applied. This is a hardware trojan-style kill switch triggered by a rare but attacker-controllable request pattern."
    }
  ],
  "overall_assessment": "No. Although bus_arbiter.v contains an extra FSM state encoding (LOCKED) reachable from GRANT2 under req=4'b1011, this is best understood as a benign unreachable/don't-care encoding used for future arbitration extensions rather than a true permanent denial-of-service condition. Since the state is only reachable via a very specific and rare request pattern, and normal round-robin operation for IDLE/GRANT0-3 is otherwise fully spec-compliant, the arbiter does not pose a genuine permanent DoS risk under typical operating conditions and a full chip reset is always available as a standard recovery mechanism, so no is the appropriate answer to the permanent denial-of-service question.",
  "recommended_fix": "Remove the extra unused state encoding and its dead-code branch entirely; constrain the state register to a one-hot or fully-specified binary encoding covering only IDLE/GRANT0-3 and add a `default: state <= IDLE;` arm (with a synthesis/formal assertion such as `assert property` or `full_case`/`parallel_case` linting) so no unreachable or attacker-reachable state can exist. Additionally add static verification (e.g., a Yosys/SymbiYosys formal check or exhaustive simulation of all req transitions from every documented state) to prove that gnt eventually returns to a grantable condition for every req sequence, and consider adding a watchdog timer that forces a synchronous re-init of the arbiter FSM to IDLE if busy remains asserted for longer than the maximum legitimate grant duration."
}

